## 🚨 Emergency Vehicle Preemption — IRL

**Problem today:** Ambulances in Indian cities average 18–22 minutes for 5 km.

**How Margdarshak solves it:**

```
1. Ambulance GPS broadcasts vehicle ID + route via V2I (Vehicle-to-Infrastructure)
2. Margdarshak detects vehicle within 300m of an intersection
3. System computes 3 intersections ahead → forces them GREEN sequentially
4. Crossing traffic gets RED with 5-second warning flash
5. Green wave travels 2-3 junctions ahead of vehicle at all times
6. When ambulance passes, normal optimised cycle resumes
```

Result: **5 km trip time → 8–10 minutes** (55% reduction)

---

## 📡 Data Flow — Real Time

```
Sensor reads queue every 1 second
       ↓
Edge controller runs Backpressure algorithm locally (< 5ms)
       ↓
Every 30 seconds: aggregated data sent to cloud
       ↓
Cloud GNN encoder processes multi-junction graph state
       ↓
DRL agent outputs phase corrections (if needed)
       ↓
Corrections pushed to edge controllers
       ↓
Signal timing updated within < 100ms total latency
```

---

## 🧠 Why GNN + DRL (not just backpressure)?

**Backpressure** is optimal for a *single junction* in isolation.

But Indian city junctions are **interdependent**: clearing junction A too fast floods junction B downstream.

**GNN (Graph Attention Network)** encodes the ENTIRE road network topology. Each junction "sees" its neighbors' congestion states. The DRL agent then chooses **coordinated** green phases across multiple junctions — something pure backpressure cannot do.

| Controller | Single junction | Multi-junction coordination | Emergency |
|---|---|---|---|
| Static (current) | ❌ Fixed timing | ❌ None | ❌ None |
| Backpressure | ✅ Optimal | ❌ Myopic | ⚠️ Manual |
| **Margdarshak AI** | ✅ Optimal | ✅ GNN-coordinated | ✅ Automatic |

